# local database snapshots (backups, pre-deploy copies) — never commit these
data/app.db.backup-*
data/app.db.*-test
data/app.db.live.*
data/app.db-shm
data/app.db-wal

# Generated invoices — the bill lives in the database, not in the repo.
out/

# Stray databases and data extracts must never be committed. dev.db carries an
# admin password hash and july.csv carries client plates and rates; both sat
# untracked and unignored, one `git add -A` away from being published.
# NOTE: this does not untrack data/app.db or service-record-data.db — .gitignore
# has no effect on files git already tracks. Removing those needs a history
# rewrite, not an ignore rule.
/dev.db
/july.csv
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# Ad-hoc database snapshots taken before a bulk change. Useful locally, never
# worth 30 MB in the history — and the repo is public.
data/*.bak-*
# Scratch copies used to rehearse a change before it touches the real file.
data/*.preview*
.next-server.log

# ── Live databases: they ship out of band, never through git ─────────────────
# These were tracked until production. A tracked database is a live hazard on a
# server: `git pull` overwrites it with the committed copy when the file is
# clean, refuses the pull when it is dirty (and the reflex `git checkout --`
# then destroys it), and a pull that includes the untrack commit deletes it
# outright. The production database therefore lives at
# /var/lib/fuel-system/app.db, OUTSIDE this tree, and these rules keep the
# local copies from being committed back by a stray `git add -A`.
# *.db above already covers most of this; named explicitly so the intent is
# readable and a future narrowing of that glob cannot quietly re-expose them.
data/app.db
data/app-ship.db
service-record-data.db
data/fuel-data-export.json
backups/
data/app-ship.db.sha256
data/new-passwords-*.txt
data/app.db.pre-rotate-*
